{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3110","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3110","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3110\",\"parent_revision\":\"32091e82228f189e0a429cdef0b14a58e52f1fd8\",\"root_revision\":\"e852410d5f3478602a4d738257b55224c696999393660293ea20289f6b717b4b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item \u2014 its deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict), not a mandate to ship a feature.\\n\\nOrigin: mechanism mined from Claude Code's built-in ultracode Workflow orchestration system \u2014 a code-executed multi-agent workflow runtime whose design contract contains several simple-but-effective patterns DH can adopt without adopting the runtime itself.\\n\\nShared design constraint (same as #3097\u2013#3103): the model keeps the judgment but writes it down once, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists. No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 out of scope.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore a dispatch journal for forensics and prefix-cached plan resume** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: ultracode Workflow orchestration system\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Why\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nBorrowed from ultracode: every run persists a journal recording each agent call's actual inputs and return value; diagnosing a run starts by reading the journal, not by guessing; and resume re-runs a workflow such that the longest unchanged prefix of agent calls returns cached results instantly \u2014 only edited or new calls run live (with determinism constraints on the orchestration layer to make replay safe). DH's equivalents are weaker on both halves: forensics for the #3060 incident required manually reconstructing the dispatch DAG from scattered agent transcripts (the incident report's proposed trace-agent-DAG methodology exists only as a spec because no journal exists to query), and the resume workflow re-establishes context by re-reading state rather than replaying a recorded dispatch history \u2014 completed dispatch work is re-derived or re-trusted, not cache-returned.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nBorrowed from ultracode: every run persists a journal recording each agent call's actual inputs and return value; diagnosing a run starts by reading the journal, not by guessing; and resume re-runs a workflow such that the longest unchanged prefix of agent calls returns cached results instantly \u2014 only edited or new calls run live (with determinism constraints on the orchestration layer to make replay safe). DH's equivalents are weaker on both halves: forensics for the #3060 incident required manually reconstructing the dispatch DAG from scattered agent transcripts (the incident report's proposed trace-agent-DAG methodology exists only as a spec because no journal exists to query), and the resume workflow re-establishes context by re-reading state rather than replaying a recorded dispatch history \u2014 completed dispatch work is re-derived or re-trusted, not cache-returned.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Mitigates\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nExpensive post-incident forensics (transcript archaeology instead of a query); inability to answer \\\"who dispatched whom, with what prompt, and what came back\\\" during a live run; wasted re-execution of already-completed dispatches when a plan run is interrupted and resumed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefit Lands In\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nIncident response and observability (the #3060 investigation cost would have been a fraction with a journal); resume fidelity and cost; and it is the data substrate several sibling items query (attempt counts #3102, the plan budget ledger from the token-budget governance item, failure events #3099).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nIncident response and observability (the #3060 investigation cost would have been a fraction with a journal); resume fidelity and cost; and it is the data substrate several sibling items query (attempt counts #3102, the plan budget ledger from the token-budget governance item (#3109), failure events #3099).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Proposed Exploration\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n1. Define the journal record (dispatch id, parent, task address, assigned role/executor-type per #3100, prompt hash or path, model/effort, timestamps, completion payload per the schema-validated completion contracts item, cost).\\n2. The natural write points are the existing dispatch path and the completion/state-transition path \u2014 no new runtime.\\n3. Journal lives with plan state (queryable via the SAM CLI/MCP).\\n4. Prototype resume-with-prefix-cache at the task level: on resume, tasks whose journal shows validated completion are cache-returned, not re-dispatched \u2014 measure re-run cost on an interrupted plan vs today's resume.\\n5. Validate forensics value by replaying the #3060 question set (the incident report's DAG-tracing spec) against a journaled run.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n1. Define the journal record (dispatch id, parent, task address, assigned role/executor-type per #3100, prompt hash or path, model/effort, timestamps, completion payload per the schema-validated completion contracts item (#3106), cost).\\n2. The natural write points are the existing dispatch path and the completion/state-transition path \u2014 no new runtime.\\n3. Journal lives with plan state (queryable via the SAM CLI/MCP).\\n4. Prototype resume-with-prefix-cache at the task level: on resume, tasks whose journal shows validated completion are cache-returned, not re-dispatched \u2014 measure re-run cost on an interrupted plan vs today's resume.\\n5. Validate forensics value by replaying the #3060 question set (the incident report's DAG-tracing spec) against a journaled run.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n#3100, #3102, #3099, #3097 (the journal is their shared evidence substrate); the schema-validated completion contracts item (#3106) (journal stores its validated payloads); the #3060 incident report's trace-agent-DAG methodology (this is its permanent home).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d14f83307aad0b81a2041408504815c0f3d51d2c1b5fe3b65a6cfb284c68ab16\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIqWJw\"}"}